 "cover": "Who came to Sumba from Europe, when, where they landed, and what they wrote down.",
 "intro": "This story follows the Europeans who reached Sumba: Portuguese and Dutch ships, the VOC acting through its ally Bima, contracts signed in Kupang, the first official at Kambaniru, the missions and their schools, and the ethnographers who lived in Sumbanese domains and wrote the books we still read. For more than two centuries Europeans claimed Sumba on paper long before they governed it. Most of the early record comes from Company letters, so Sumbanese voices reach us at second hand. Where sources give different dates, the slide says so. Places marked \"approximate\" are placed by district, not surveyed.",
 "slides": [
  {
   "id": "s01",
   "label": "c. 1512–1600",
   "title": "Known by rumour, named for sandalwood",
   "text": "Soelarto reports that the first Europeans Sumbanese remembered were Portuguese traders, followed later by the English. Europeans called the island Sandalwood Island, Pulau Cendana. Hägerdal points out that the name misled them: when the island was properly examined it turned out to be a poor source of sandalwood. For a century Sumba sat at the edge of European charts, approached from Solor, Flores and Timor rather than visited for itself.",
   "more": "",
